      "role": "user",
      "content": "A block moves along a rough horizontal surface while a horizontal force F of magnitude 18 N and a constant kinetic frictional force of magnitude 6 N act on it. Which ONE of the following combinations of ACCELERATION and NET WORK DONE ON THE BLOCK is CORRECT?\n\n| | ACCELERATION | NET WORK DONE ON THE BLOCK |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | Constant | Increases |\n| B | Increases | Constant |\n| C | Increases | Increases |\n| D | Constant | Constant |"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Student needs to determine net force first. Ask for net force to diagnose sign error. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"18 - 6\") -> 12]",
      "content": "To answer this, we first need to find the net force acting on the block. The applied force is 18 N to the right, and the kinetic frictional force is 6 N to the left. What is the net force acting on the block? (Include direction.)"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Net force = 18 N + 6 N = 24 N to the right. So acceleration increases and net work increases. Answer C."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Learner added instead of subtracting. Correct the sign error, then explain constant net force means constant acceleration, and net work increases with displacement. Final answer A.",
      "content": "You added the forces, but friction opposes motion, so it acts to the left. The net force is $18 - 6 = 12\\text{ N}$ to the right. Since this net force is constant, the acceleration is constant (Newton's second law: $a = F_{\\text{net}}/m$). The block keeps moving, so displacement increases, and net work $W_{\\text{net}} = F_{\\text{net}} \\Delta x$ increases. Therefore the correct combination is **A**.\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $F_{\\text{net}} = ma$ and $W_{\\text{net}} = F_{\\text{net}} \\Delta x$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $F_{\\text{net}}$: the total force after adding all forces (taking direction into account)\n  * $m$: mass of the block\n  * $a$: acceleration\n  * $W_{\\text{net}}$: net work done on the block\n  * $\\Delta x$: displacement of the block\n* **Why we use it:** Newton's second law tells us that constant net force gives constant acceleration. Net work is force times displacement, so if force is constant and displacement grows, work grows.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always subtract opposing forces (like friction) from the applied force to get net force. Remember that constant net force means constant acceleration, not increasing acceleration."
    }
  ],
